    "# QP-001: Advanced Quantum Processor Simulator\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook demonstrates a more advanced quantum processor simulation workflow using Qiskit and PennyLane. It covers:\n",
    "- Bell-state preparation\n",
    "- GHZ-state generation\n",
    "- Noise modeling\n",
    "- Quantum error correction with repetition code\n",
    "- Basic processor abstraction for experiment orchestration\n",
    "\n",
    "The goal is to move from simple examples to a cleaner and more reusable quantum processor simulation project.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Install and verify required packages\n",
    "!python -m pip install --quiet qiskit qiskit-aer pennylane numpy matplotlib seaborn\n",
    "\n",
    "import sys\n",
    "import json\n",
    "import math\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "\n",
    "import qiskit\n",
    "import qiskit_aer\n",
    "import pennylane as qml\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Python:\", sys.version.split()[0])\n",
    "print(\"Qiskit:\", qiskit.__version__)\n",
    "print(\"Qiskit Aer:\", qiskit_aer.__version__)\n",
    "print(\"PennyLane:\", qml.__version__)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1) Bell-state simulation with Qiskit\n",
    "\n",
    "This is a foundational quantum state where two qubits are entangled. The two valid outcomes are \"00\" and \"11\" with equal probability.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from qiskit import QuantumCircuit\n",
    "from qiskit_aer import AerSimulator\n",
    "\n",
    "bell = QuantumCircuit(2, 2)\n",
    "bell.h(0)\n",
    "bell.cx(0, 1)\n",
    "bell.measure([0, 1], [0, 1])\n",
    "bell.draw(output=\"mpl\", style=\"fivethirtyeight\")\n",
    "\n",
    "sim = AerSimulator()\n",
    "result = sim.run(bell, shots=1024).result()\n",
    "counts = result.get_counts()\n",
    "print(\"Bell measurement counts:\", counts)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Visualize distribution\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(6, 4))\n",
    "plt.bar(counts.keys(), counts.values(), color=[\"#4C72B0\", \"#55A868\"])\n",
    "plt.title(\"Bell State Measurement Distribution\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Outcome\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Counts\")\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2) GHZ processor experiment\n",
    "\n",
    "The Greenberger-Horne-Zeilinger (GHZ) state creates a highly entangled system. For an 8-qubit GHZ state, the system should be dominated by \"00000000\" and \"11111111\" outcomes with equal probability.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def ghz_state(n_qubits=8):\n",
    "    qc = QuantumCircuit(n_qubits, n_qubits)\n",
    "    qc.h(0)\n",
    "    for i in range(n_qubits - 1):\n",
    "        qc.cx(i, i + 1)\n",
    "    qc.measure(range(n_qubits), range(n_qubits))\n",
    "    return qc\n",
    "\n",
    "n = 8\n",
    "qc_ghz = ghz_state(n)\n",
    "print(qc_ghz)\n",
    "\n",
    "sim = AerSimulator()\n",
    "result = sim.run(qc_ghz, shots=4096).result()\n",
    "counts = result.get_counts()\n",
    "\n",
    "filtered = {k: v for k, v in counts.items() if v > 10}\n",
    "print(\"Important GHZ outcomes:\", filtered)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Show all significant outcomes\n",
    "labels = list(filtered.keys())\n",
    "values = list(filtered.values())\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))\n",
    "plt.bar(labels, values, color=\"#DD8452\")\n",
    "plt.title(f\"GHZ State (n={n}) Measurement Outcomes\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Bitstring\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Counts\")\n",
    "plt.xticks(rotation=45)\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3) PennyLane: parameterized circuit and gradient\n",
    "\n",
    "PennyLane is useful for differentiable quantum circuits and hybrid optimization workflows.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "dev = qml.device(\"default.qubit\", wires=1)\n",
    "\n",
    "@qml.qnode(dev, shots=None)\n",
    "def circuit(phi1, phi2):\n",
    "    qml.RX(phi1, wires=0)\n",
    "    qml.RY(phi2, wires=0)\n",
    "    return qml.expval(qml.PauliZ(0))\n",
    "\n",
    "def cost(x, y):\n",
    "    return np.sin(np.abs(circuit(x, y))) - 1\n",
    "\n",
    "phi1 = np.array(0.5, requires_grad=True)\n",
    "phi2 = np.array(0.5, requires_grad=True)\n",
    "dcost = qml.grad(cost, argnums=[0, 1])\n",
    "gradient = dcost(phi1, phi2)\n",
    "print(\"Gradient:\", gradient)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4) Noise simulation\n",
    "\n",
    "Noisy quantum devices are a practical reality. Here we simulate a depolarizing error model on a GHZ processor.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "n = 8\n",
    "noise_level = 0.01\n",
    "shots = 2000\n",
    "\n",
    "dev_noisy = qml.device(\"default.mixed\", wires=n)\n",
    "\n",
    "@qml.qnode(dev_noisy, shots=shots)\n",
    "def noisy_ghz_processor():\n",
    "    qml.Hadamard(wires=0)\n",
    "    for i in range(n - 1):\n",
    "        qml.CNOT(wires=[i, i + 1])\n",
    "        qml.DepolarizingChannel(noise_level, wires=i)\n",
    "        qml.DepolarizingChannel(noise_level, wires=i + 1)\n",
    "    return qml.sample(wires=range(n))\n",
    "\n",
    "samples = noisy_ghz_processor()\n",
    "states, counts = np.unique(samples, axis=0, return_counts=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "measurement_counts = {\n",
    "    \"\".join(map(str, state)): int(count) for state, count in zip(states, counts)\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "zero_state = \"0\" * n\n",
    "one_state = \"1\" * n\n",
    "zero_count = measurement_counts.get(zero_state, 0)\n",
    "one_count = measurement_counts.get(one_state, 0)\n",
    "ghz_count = zero_count + one_count\n",
    "ghz_percentage = (ghz_count / shots) * 100\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Noise level:\", noise_level)\n",
    "print(\"Shots:\", shots)\n",
    "print(\"Zero-state count:\", zero_count)\n",
    "print(\"One-state count:\", one_count)\n",
    "print(\"GHZ-state percentage:\", round(ghz_percentage, 2), \"%\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Print top error outcomes\n",
    "for bitstring, count in sorted(measurement_counts.items(), key=lambda item: item[1], reverse=True)[:10]:\n",
    "    print(f\"{bitstring}: {count}\")\n"
   ]
  },
